#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112e5b58-a689-5138-b33f-e47c56e7167c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482839} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc280e-d5f1-5a84-9cfc-9c3ad50813fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482920} {:answer/id #uuid "7228d70a-0771-5134-8636-ee3ba6b53f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483011} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e6b23f-d37b-563e-840d-2a93839faacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483179} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bafb54-6e90-57a9-943c-bbd0971097f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "112e5b58-a689-5138-b33f-e47c56e7167c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482839} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d3adf1-3aa8-506a-a4e2-98cf256eb5b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4126f1c5-7567-585d-9e49-d37c72e8e18c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162753" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4x-2y+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,1),\\,(2,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(2,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-9$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1),$ and its minimum value is $-9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f53dcb-0e32-5ceb-8aaf-a210c8dc8521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278823} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9bf5ef-0b2f-58e3-a02b-38b0b94d9f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278848} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f417c7-1adf-5968-be5e-7a28cd0f3ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280219} {:answer/id #uuid "c67face3-ea0f-54ec-abf8-31d98bb676cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280331} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e1ed97-fd98-5ee9-abbe-fa7c46a9977b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f53dcb-0e32-5ceb-8aaf-a210c8dc8521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278823} :answer-field/id #uuid "40847485-fda7-51d7-b82e-0e2ddf1864ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "932d87ff-4a97-5b0b-b8d7-da2ba43e7f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162763" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4y-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,4-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4-2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2) &=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1) &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\sqrt{5}),(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-2+1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(\\pm 1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-\\sqrt{5})$ | $(0,\\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 1,-2)$ and its minimum value is $-14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e57bce-4288-5fe3-aa0a-73628c52743d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278746} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3b843e-b72a-51a2-a995-da57e46e487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279446} {:answer/id #uuid "81d5aaa0-fd9f-568f-9bb1-e082fcdaa7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279754} {:answer/id #uuid "a999c752-b365-5d3b-960d-66d2a2fbec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280097} {:answer/id #uuid "ced37650-ae67-5a4c-bc4a-bb74c5223c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d5aaa0-fd9f-568f-9bb1-e082fcdaa7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279754} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be271dc-3c10-5641-8030-505366b398d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76775e9c-a3b9-5240-b988-cfc2af339188" :question/math-academy-id "q-162764" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2+1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0),(-2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-3$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 2)$ and its minimum value is $-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba6dc59a-d58e-52fa-ab73-eb12adb3b9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123255} {:answer/id #uuid "e0be39ec-ebe1-5490-8c20-efd23eff06dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123256} {:answer/id #uuid "0469e86f-4896-589f-be98-7d2dcb9b622a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123257} {:answer/id #uuid "66fdae75-3fd9-5769-80cc-77dba87e1ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123258} {:answer/id #uuid "72fceb0d-f6b0-5e5c-9ecb-2fc5b7e2e60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0469e86f-4896-589f-be98-7d2dcb9b622a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123257} :answer-field/id #uuid "53789dcc-b6e4-5a59-b807-fa2867d7d6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4948ed57-4d3d-575d-8636-517fc5219397" :question/math-academy-id "q-37869" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+3y\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=13.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=13.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=13\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=13$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =13 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,-3),\\,(2,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,-3)$ | $(2,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-13$ | $13$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,3),$ and its maximum value is $13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1502cf5f-a5dc-5e05-9215-eccfb24f8048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435009} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1539e6-873b-5a28-a445-ae8ed6fd0bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435010} {:answer/id #uuid "71ec4d29-d08a-51b3-8502-091c7326e596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435011} {:answer/id #uuid "723c1aad-7a95-5932-9d73-d4da0d9899fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435012} {:answer/id #uuid "afb0c189-c8eb-5d7d-9361-30bdbdc56df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1502cf5f-a5dc-5e05-9215-eccfb24f8048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435009} :answer-field/id #uuid "73c99f01-9858-5d97-8611-2fe539762d19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24aab5f2-d53f-5124-b5bc-fe883979c5fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-99037" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=25-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=\\lambda (2y-4)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1+\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(1+\\lambda ) &=2\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0,$ the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0),(0,4).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1,$ from the second equation of the system we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1-1)=2(-1)\\,⇒\\,0=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,4).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $25$ | $9$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,0),$ and its maximum value is $25.$"}]}
